       | \[
       | \mathcal{F}= \{\alpha:\mathbb Z\to\mathbb Z\mid \alpha (n)=0\text{ for all but finitely many }n\},
       | \qquad 
       | (\alpha\star\beta)=\sum_{n\in\mathbb Z}\alpha (n)\beta (n).
       | \]
       | 
       | For a shift \(S_{t}\) we have  
       | 
       | \[
       | (S_{t}\alpha)\star\beta=\sum_{n}\alpha (n+t)\beta (n)
       |       =\sum_{n}\alpha (n)\beta (n-t).
       | \]
       | 
       | Hence, if we write the generating polynomials  
       | 
       | \[
       | A(x)=\sum_{n\in\mathbb Z}\alpha (n)x^{n},\qquad 
       | B(x)=\sum_{n\in\mathbb Z}\beta (n)x^{n},
       | \]
       | 
       | then  
       | 
       | \[
       | (S_{t}\alpha)\star\beta =\text{coefficient of }x^{t}\text{ in }A(x)B(x^{-1}).
       | \]
       | 
       | Thus the condition in the definition of a *shifty* function is equivalent to  
       | 
       | \[
       | A(x)C(x)=x^{k}+x^{\ell}\qquad(\ell\neq k)          \tag{1}
       | \]
       | 
       | for some Laurent polynomial \(C(x)=x^{s}B(x^{-1})\) with integer
       | coefficients.  
       | Writing \(A(x)=x^{r}A_{0}(x)\) where \(r\) is the smallest exponent
       | with a non‑zero coefficient, (1) becomes  
       | 
       | \[
       | A_{0}(x)D(x)=1+x^{d}\qquad(d=\ell-k>0)                \tag{2}
       | \]
       | 
       | with \(D(x)=x^{k-r}C(x)\).  
       | Thus a polynomial \(A_{0}(x)\) (with non‑zero constant term) must be a
       | divisor of a polynomial of the form \(1+x^{d}\).
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | ### 1.  Divisors of \(1+x^{d}\)
       | 
       | For any \(d\ge 1\),
       | 
       | \[
       | 1+x^{d}= \prod_{\substack{e\mid 2d\\ e\nmid d}}\Phi _e(x),
       | \]
       | 
       | where \(\Phi _e\) is the \(e\)-th cyclotomic polynomial.
       | If \(d\) is odd, the set of indices in the product is  
       | \(\{\,2e\mid e\mid d\,\}\); if \(d\) is even, writing \(d=2^{v}O\)
       | with \(O\) odd, the indices are  
       | 
       | \[
       | \{\,2^{\,v+1}o\mid o\mid O\,\}.
       | \]
       | 
       | Hence **all the cyclotomic factors occurring in a divisor of \(1+x^{d}\)
       | have the same 2‑adic valuation** (the exponent of the factor \(2\) in
       | their index).  Conversely, for any integer \(t\ge1\) and any odd integer
       | \(O\ge1\),
       | 
       | \[
       | 1+x^{\,2^{\,t-1}O}= \prod_{o\mid O}\Phi_{\,2^{t}o}(x),
       | \]
       | 
       | so any product of cyclotomic polynomials whose indices all have the same
       | 2‑adic valuation \(t\) does divide some \(1+x^{d}\).
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | ### 2.  Relevant cyclotomic polynomials
       | 
       | Only cyclotomic polynomials of degree at most \(8\) can appear, because
       | \(\alpha\) is required to vanish outside \(\{0,1,\dots ,8\}\).
       | The even‑indexed cyclotomic polynomials with degree \(\le8\) are  
       | 
       | \[
       | \begin{array}{c|c|c}
       | d & \Phi_d(x) & \deg\Phi_d\\ \hline
       | 2 & x+1 & 1\\
       | 4 & x^{2}+1 & 2\\
       | 6 & x^{2}-x+1 & 2\\
       | 8 & x^{4}+1 & 4\\
       | 10& x^{4}-x^{3}+x^{2}-x+1 & 4\\
       | 12& x^{4}-x^{2}+1 & 4\\
       | 14& x^{6}-x^{5}+x^{4}-x^{3}+x^{2}-x+1 & 6\\
       | 16& x^{8}+1 & 8\\
       | 18& x^{6}-x^{3}+1 & 6\\
       | 20& x^{8}-x^{6}+x^{4}-x^{2}+1 & 8\\
       | 24& x^{8}-x^{4}+1 & 8\\
       | 30& x^{8}+x^{7}-x^{5}-x^{4}-x^{3}+x+1 & 8
       | \end{array}
       | \]
       | 
       | Their 2‑adic valuations are  
       | 
       | \[
       | \begin{array}{c|c}
       | t & \text{indices with }v_{2}=t\\ \hline
       | 1 & 2,6,10,14,18,30\\
       | 2 & 4,12,20\\
       | 3 & 8,24\\
       | 4 & 16
       | \end{array}
       | \]
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | ### 3.  All possible \(A_{0}\)
       | 
       | \(A_{0}\) must be a product of a **subset of one of the four rows above**
       | (the subsets may be empty) and its total degree must not exceed \(8\).
       | Enumerating the admissible subsets gives  
