       | 
       | \[
       | 1+x^{d}= \prod_{\substack{m\mid d\\ m\text{ odd}}}\Phi_{2m}(x),
       | \]
       | 
       | where \(\Phi_{2m}\) is the cyclotomic polynomial of order \(2m\)
       | (\(\Phi_{2}(x)=x+1\), \(\Phi_{6}(x)=x^{2}-x+1\), …).
       | Hence every divisor of \(1+x^{d}\) is a product of **distinct**
       | \(\Phi_{2m}(x)\) with odd \(m\), possibly multiplied by \(\pm1\).
       | 
       | The degree of \(\Phi_{2m}\) is \(\varphi(m)\) (Euler’s totient).
       | Only odd \(m\) with \(\varphi(m)\le 8\) can appear, otherwise the total
       | degree would exceed the allowed degree \(8\) of \(A\).
       | 
       | \[
       | \begin{array}{c|c}
       | m & \varphi(m)\\ \hline
       | 1 & 1\\
       | 3 & 2\\
       | 5 & 4\\
       | 7 & 6\\
       | 9 & 6\\
       | 15& 8
       | \end{array}
       | \]
       | 
       | Thus the possible cyclotomic factors are
       | \(\Phi_{2},\Phi_{6},\Phi_{10},\Phi_{14},\Phi_{18},\Phi_{30}\).
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | ### 3.  All admissible divisors \(C\)
       | 
       | Let \(M=\{1,3,5,7,9,15\}\).
       | For any subset \(S\subseteq M\) the product  
       | 
       | \[
       | C_S(x)=\prod_{m\in S}\Phi_{2m}(x)
       | \]
       | 
       | has degree \(\displaystyle\deg C_S=\sum_{m\in S}\varphi(m)\).
       | We need \(\deg C_S\le 8\).
       | The subsets satisfying this are
       | 
       | \[
       | \begin{aligned}
       | &\varnothing,\{1\},\{3\},\{5\},\{7\},\{9\},\{15\},\\
       | &\{1,3\},\{1,5\},\{1,7\},\{1,9\},\{3,5\},\\
