       | assistantfinalLet the side length of the cube be \(s\) metres.
       | Choose a Cartesian system with the horizontal plane \(\mathcal P\) as the
       | \(xy\)-plane (\(z=0\)).
       | Let  
       | 
       | \[
       | \mathbf u,\mathbf v,\mathbf w
       | \]
       | 
       | be the three mutually orthogonal unit vectors along the edges of the
       | cube, and let the vertex \(A\) be at the origin.
       | Then  
       | 
       | \[
       | \begin{aligned}
       | B&=s\mathbf u,\\
       | C&=s(\mathbf v+\mathbf w),\\
       | D&=s(\mathbf u+\mathbf v+\mathbf w).
       | \end{aligned}
       | \]
       | 
       | Denote the vertical component (the \(z\)–coordinate) of a vector
       | \(\mathbf x\) by \(\mathbf x\cdot\mathbf k\) where \(\mathbf k\) is the unit
       | vertical vector.  Put  
       | 
       | \[
       | a=\mathbf u\!\cdot\!\mathbf k ,\qquad
       | b=\mathbf v\!\cdot\!\mathbf k ,\qquad
       | c=\mathbf w\!\cdot\!\mathbf k .
       | \]
       | 
       | Because the edges have length \(s\),
       | 
       | \[
       | s a =2,\qquad s(b+c)=8,\qquad s(a+b+c)=10 .
       | \tag{1}
       | \]
       | 
       | Hence  
       | 
       | \[
       | a=\frac{2}{s},\qquad b+c=\frac{8}{s},\qquad a+b+c=\frac{10}{s}.
       | \tag{2}
       | \]
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | ### The perpendicular condition
       | 
       | The rectangle \(ABDC\) is spanned by the vectors \(\overrightarrow{AB}=s\mathbf u\)
       | and \(\overrightarrow{BD}=s(\mathbf v+\mathbf w)\); its normal vector is  
       | 
       | \[
       | \mathbf n =\overrightarrow{AB}\times\overrightarrow{BD}
       |           =s^{2}\bigl(\mathbf u\times\mathbf v+\mathbf u\times\mathbf w\bigr)
       |           =s^{2}(\mathbf w-\mathbf v).
       | \]
       | 
       | For the plane of the rectangle to be **perpendicular** to the horizontal
       | plane \(\mathcal P\), its normal must be horizontal, i.e. its vertical
       | component must be zero.  The vertical component of \(\mathbf n\) is
       | \(c-b\); therefore  
       | 
       | \[
       | c-b=0\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad b=c .
       | \tag{3}
       | \]
       | 
       | From (2) and (3) we obtain  
       | 
       | \[
       | b=c=\frac{4}{s},\qquad a=\frac{2}{s}.
       | \]
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | ### Determining the side length
       | 
       | The three edge‑direction vectors are orthonormal, so  
       | 
       | \[
       | a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}=1 .
       | \]
       | 
       | Substituting the expressions for \(a,b,c\),
       | 
       | \[
       | \left(\frac{2}{s}\right)^{2}+2\left(\frac{4}{s}\right)^{2}=1
       | \Longrightarrow\frac{4+32}{s^{2}}=1
       | \Longrightarrow s^{2}=36\;,
       | \]
       | 
       | hence  
       | 
       | \[
       | \boxed{s=6\text{ m}} .
       | \]
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | 

