       | 
       | denote the position in which the long hand points to the number \(i\) and the short hand points to the number \(j\) (the numbers are taken modulo \(12\)).  
       | From a position \(P_{i,j}\) a single move can be
       | 
       | * **H** : the long hand moves clockwise one step → \(P_{i+1,j}\);
       | * **V** : the short hand moves clockwise one step → \(P_{i,j+1}\).
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | ### 1.  From a sequence of moves to a functional digraph  
       | 
       | Write  
       | 
       | \[
       | x_{i,j}= \begin{cases}
       | 0 &\text{if the move from }P_{i,j}\text{ is H (east)},\\[2mm]
       | 1 &\text{if the move from }P_{i,j}\text{ is V (north)} .
       | \end{cases}
       | \]
       | 
       | Thus each vertex \(P_{i,j}\) has exactly one outgoing edge, namely  
       | 
       | \[
       | P_{i,j}\longrightarrow 
       | \begin{cases}
       | P_{i+1,j}& (x_{i,j}=0),\\
       | P_{i,j+1}& (x_{i,j}=1).
       | \end{cases}
       | \tag{1}
       | \]
       | 
       | Because the walk has to visit every vertex exactly once and return to the
       | starting vertex, the directed graph defined by the \(x_{i,j}\)’s must be a **single directed cycle** (a Hamiltonian cycle) that contains all \(12\cdot12=144\) vertices.
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | ### 2.  The indegree condition  
       | 
       | If a vertex \(P_{i,j}\) is entered, it must be entered from exactly one neighbour,
       | otherwise some vertex would have indegree \(0\) or \(2\) and the walk could not be a
       | single cycle.  
       | The only possible predecessors of \(P_{i,j}\) are  
       | 
       | \[
       | P_{i-1,j}\;( \text{if }x_{i-1,j}=0),\qquad 
       | P_{i,j-1}\;( \text{if }x_{i,j-1}=1).
       | \]
       | 
       | Hence we need
       | 
       | \[
       | x_{i,j-1}=x_{i-1,j}\qquad\text{for all }i,j\;( \bmod 12). \tag{2}
       | \]
       | 
       | Equation (2) is a linear system over the field \(\mathbf{F}_2\).
       | Writing the \(144\) variables \(x_{i,j}\) in a column vector,
       | the coefficient matrix has rank \(144-12\); therefore the solution space
       | has dimension \(12\).  Consequently there are  
       | 
       | \[
       | 2^{12}=4096
       | \]
       | 
       | different assignments \(\{x_{i,j}\}\) that satisfy the indegree‑outdegree
       | condition (2).  Every such assignment gives a functional digraph in which
       | each vertex has indegree = outdegree = 1; the digraph consists of a disjoint
       | union of directed cycles.
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | ### 3.  When is the digraph a single cycle?  
       | 
       | Let \(A\) be the \(144\times144\) matrix whose \((i,j)\)-entry is \(1\) if
       | \(P_i\) can go to \(P_j\) (i.e. if the move is east or north) and \(0\) otherwise.
       | For a fixed assignment \(\{x_{i,j}\}\) the corresponding permutation matrix
       | \(P\) is obtained from \(A\) by keeping exactly one ‘1’ in each row (the one
       | chosen by the value of \(x_{i,j}\)).  
       | 
       | The number of such permutation matrices that consist of **exactly one cycle**
       | is given by the BEST theorem (de Bruijn‑van Aardenne‑Ehrenfest‑Smith‑Tutte)
       | applied to the directed Eulerian graph whose adjacency matrix is \(A\):
