       | \]
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | ### 1.  From a set of edges to a permutation
       | 
       | Choose for every vertex which of its two outgoing edges will be used.
       | Denote  
       | 
       | \[
       | x_{i,j}= \begin{cases}
       | 1 &\text{if the edge }(i,j)\to(i+1,j)\text{ (east) is chosen},\\
       | 0 &\text{if the edge }(i,j)\to(i,j+1)\text{ (north) is chosen}.
       | \end{cases}
       | \]
       | 
       | The chosen edges must give a **bijection** of \(V\); i.e. each vertex must have
       | exactly one incoming edge as well as one outgoing edge.
       | 
       | A vertex \((i,j)\) receives an incoming edge either from \((i-1,j)\) (east) or
       | from \((i,j-1)\) (north), but not from both.  
       | Hence for every \((i,j)\)
       | 
       | \[
       | x_{i-1,j}=x_{i,j-1}. \tag{1}
       | \]
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | ### 2.  Solving the indegree condition
       | 
       | Equation (1) says that the values of \(x\) are equal on the two neighbours that
       | lie on the same diagonal \(i+j\equiv s\pmod{12}\).  
       | Consequently all vertices whose coordinates satisfy \(i+j\equiv s\) have the
       | same value.  
       | 
       | Thus there are 12 independent binary variables  
       | 
       | \[
       | d_s\in\{0,1\},\qquad s=0,1,\dots ,11,
       | \]
       | 
       | and  
       | 
       | \[
       | x_{i,j}=d_{\,i+j\pmod{12}} . \tag{2}
       | \]
       | 
       | Every choice of the 12‑tuple \((d_0,\dots ,d_{11})\) satisfies the indegree
       | condition, so it defines a permutation \(f\) of the 144 vertices:
       | \[
       | f(i,j)=\begin{cases}
       | (i+1,j) &\text{if }d_{i+j}=1,\\[2mm]
       | (i,j+1) &\text{if }d_{i+j}=0 .
       | \end{cases}
       | \]
       | 
