       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | ### 1.  Families that contain a singleton  
       | 
       | If a family \(\mathcal F\) contains a singleton \(\{i\}\), then every other
       | set of \(\mathcal F\) must contain the element \(i\); otherwise it would be
       | disjoint from \(\{i\}\).
       | Hence \(\mathcal F\) is forced to be the **star**
       | \[
       | \mathcal F_i=\{\,X\subseteq\{1,\dots ,5\}\mid i\in X\,\},
       | \qquad |\,\mathcal F_i|=2^{4}=16 .
       | \]
       | There are \(5\) possible centres \(i\).  
       | Thus we obtain \(5\) intersecting families.
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | ### 2.  Families with no singleton  
       | 
       | Assume now that \(\mathcal F\) contains no set of size \(1\) (and of course
       | no empty set).  
       | Then every set of size \(4\) must belong to \(\mathcal F\); otherwise its
       | complement, a singleton, would be missing and we could add it, contradicting
       | \(|\mathcal F|=16\).  
       | Consequently the whole set \(\{1,2,3,4,5\}\) (size \(5\)) also belongs to
       | \(\mathcal F\).
       | 
       | Hence \(\mathcal F\) already contains  
       | 
       | * the five \(4\)-element subsets,
       | * the one \(5\)-element subset,
       | 
       | leaving \(10\) further members to be chosen among the \(2\)- and \(3\)-element
       | subsets.
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | ### 3.  Choosing the remaining \(10\) sets  
       | 
       | For each unordered pair \(\{a,b\}\) (a \(2\)-set) its complement
       | \(\{a,b\}^c\) is the \(3\)-set \(\{c,d,e\}\).
       | These two are disjoint, while any other \(2\)- or \(3\)-set meets both of them.
       | Therefore from each complementary pair \(\bigl(\{a,b\},\{a,b\}^c\bigr)\) we may
       | choose **at most one** of the two sets.
       | 
       | Since we need exactly ten sets, we must choose **exactly one** member from each
       | of the ten complementary pairs.  
       | Let us decide for which pairs we take the \(2\)-set; the remaining pairs then
       | contribute their complementary \(3\)-sets.
       | 
       | Two chosen \(2\)-sets must intersect, otherwise they would be disjoint.
       | Thus the collection of selected \(2\)-sets must be an intersecting family of
       | edges of the complete graph \(K_5\).
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | ### 4.  Intersecting families of edges of \(K_5\)
       | 
       | Denote by \(N_k\) the number of intersecting families of \(k\) edges
       | (\(2\)-element subsets) of \(\{1,\dots ,5\}\).
       | 
       | * \(k=0\): the empty family – \(N_0=1\).
       | * \(k=1\): any edge – \(N_1=\binom{5}{2}=10\).
       | * \(k=2\): two edges must share a vertex.
       |   Choose the common vertex (\(5\) ways) and then choose the two other
       |   vertices (\(\binom{4}{2}=6\) ways): \(N_2=5\cdot6=30\).
       | * \(k=3\): either a star of three edges or a triangle.
       |   – Star: choose a centre (\(5\) ways) and three of the four incident edges
       |     (\(\binom{4}{3}=4\)): \(5\cdot4=20\).
       |   – Triangle: choose three vertices (\(\binom{5}{3}=10\)):
       |     \(10\) families.
       |   Hence \(N_3=20+10=30\).
       | * \(k=4\): the only possibility is the star of all four edges incident to a
       |   vertex, giving \(N_4=5\).
       | 
       | Thus the number of admissible choices of the ten remaining sets is  
       | 
       | \[
       | \sum_{k=0}^{4} N_k = 1+10+30+30+5 = 76 .
       | \]
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | ### 5.  Total number of families
       | 
       | Adding the five star families found in step 1 gives  
       | 
       | \[
       | 5 \;(\text{star families})\;+\;76 \;(\text{families without singletons})
       | = 81 .
       | \]
       | 
       | --------------------------------------------------------------------
       | \[
       | \boxed{81}
       | \]

