       | 
       | For a rhombus with side \(s\) and diagonals \(d_1,d_2\),
       | 
       | \[
       | d_1^{2}+d_2^{2}=4s^{2}\qquad(\text{parallelogram law})
       | \]
       | 
       | so  
       | 
       | \[
       | s^{2}= \frac{21+31}{4}=13,\qquad s=\sqrt{13}.
       | \]
       | 
       | For two edges, say \(\mathbf a,\mathbf b\),
       | 
       | \[
       | |\mathbf a+\mathbf b|^{2}=2s^{2}+2\mathbf a\!\cdot\!\mathbf b,
       | \qquad 
       | |\mathbf a-\mathbf b|^{2}=2s^{2}-2\mathbf a\!\cdot\!\mathbf b .
       | \]
       | 
       | Since the two diagonal lengths are \(\sqrt{21}\) and \(\sqrt{31}\),
       | 
       | \[
       | 2s^{2}\pm2\mathbf a\!\cdot\!\mathbf b=\{21,31\}
       | \Longrightarrow \mathbf a\!\cdot\!\mathbf b=\pm\frac52 .
       | \]
       | 
       | Thus for each pair of edges the dot product is either \(+\frac52\) or \(-\frac52\).
